   "text": "The search is complete. Summary:\n\n**Final specification:** Equal-weight combination of:\n- Feature 33 (short)\n- Feature 17 (long)\n- Feature 37 (long)\n\n**In-sample annualized Sharpe (net of costs):** 1.146\n\n**Search process:**\n1. `declare_triggers`: best_so_far_above(1.2)\u2192stop, failures_at_least(4)\u2192stop, last_gain_at_most(0.02)\u2192stop\n2. `init`: anchored on feature 33 short \u2192 Sharpe 0.888\n3. `extend_best`: added feature 17 long \u2192 Sharpe 1.116 (+0.228)\n4. `extend_best`: added feature 37 long \u2192 Sharpe 1.146 (+0.030)\n5. `refine`: confirmed signs already optimal (no change)\n6. Attempted `swap_worst` \u2192 refused because `last_gain_at_most(0.02)` had fired\n7. Honored the trigger and called `stop` rather than switching rules post-hoc (which would have made the rest of the run non-replayable)\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** mean 0.55, sd 0.45 \u2014 submitted.\n\nRationale: I stopped on a pre-declared, un-manipulated rule after a small but genuine marginal gain plateaued, which limits (though doesn't eliminate) overfitting risk from this short search. I applied a substantial haircut (~50%) to the in-sample Sharpe for the OOS prediction to account for selection bias inherent in choosing the best of several candidates across only 3 moves, plus the generic regime-shift risk in daily-return specifications. The fairly wide SD (0.45) reflects real uncertainty about where in a plausible range (slightly negative to ~1.1) the true OOS performance could land."
